  "answer": "**Verdict**\nNomex is **not safe** in the Artemis Exploration Atmosphere (34% $\\text{O}_2$, 8.2 psia) across low ventilation flows. The agreed fans-off flow is not defined yet, but the flammability boundary map shows that at low ventilation flows (0.5 cm/s to 40 cm/s), the limiting oxygen concentration drops as low as 25.8% $\\text{O}_2$ (90% interval 24.6%–27.8% at 8 cm/s). Because the Artemis atmosphere is 34% $\\text{O}_2$, it sits above the flammability limit and will burn.\n\n**Key numbers**\n- Atmosphere: 34% $\\text{O}_2$, 8.2 psia (56.5 kPa)\n- Minimum $\\text{O}_2$ limit: 25.8% $\\text{O}_2$ (90% interval range 25.8%–34.9% across the flammable flow range 1 cm/s to 40 cm/s)\n- Flammable flow range: 1 cm/s to 40 cm/s\n\n**Evidence**\n- Supporting experiment IDs: `MOCK-BASS-II-24`, `MOCK-BASS-II-18`, `MOCK-BASS-II-12`\n\n**Operational recommendation**\nLimit the amount of exposed material, isolate ignition sources, lower oxygen where the mission allows, and keep fire detection and suppression ready. Never rely on extreme low-flow quenching to prevent fires.\n\n**Limits**\n- The numbers come from a placeholder model (`mock_api`), not NASA data.\n- This is decision support, not a replacement for NASA material testing.",
